so what i'd like to do is impart to you the importance of crypto: not 
crypto as in cryptocurrency, but crypto as in cryptology. i'll do this 
through 3 stories. they will not be long. 

story #1 involves cuba, russia, and spying. but i need to start with a puzzle 
underlying the story. i'm going to tell you some digits, and you're 
going to tell me what's unusual about them... 

   1, 2, 1, 2,   3, 4, 3, 4,    5, 6, 5, 6,   7, 8, 7, 8,    3, 2, 1 

so? ... maybe it's not so much the pattern, but what's not there? ... 
right!: no 9s! the point is, that sequence is too special to be random. 
more to the point, a sequence like that is what gave away a group of 
spies on the east coast of the us in the 1980s. 
has anyone seen the tv series ``the americans''? this case is actually 
a real event that helped inspire the series. 

spies obviously need to hear from home base, and send messages to home 
base. this is where crypto comes in: (crypto as in cryptology as in 
secret codes) there are simple, standard ways to take a message, 
convert it to numbers, take a key (a long string of numbers), add the 
key to your message, and then send the new numbers to your home base. 
no one seeing the number you send would be able to know your original 
message, unless they know your key. if you are a good spy, this is true, 
and your home base knows your key. this (slow) method is known as a one-time 
pad. if you watched ``the americans'', you'll remember that philip and 
elizabeth (russian spies passing as average, upper middle-class americans) 
have a secret cubby in their garage where they have a little notebook 
storing their keys. if your memory is really good, you'll also remember 
that elizabeth would sometimes listen to the radio, to listen for strings 
of numbers. this is actually real: the russians would use shortwave radio 
to transmit numbers that spies could write down to use as keys. 

so how is this secret, if the us already knew about this for years? 
anyone? ... yes: time! spies would be told ahead of time (and through 
encrypted messages) when to listen. now... would it make sense to 
only send numbers when needed, and then go quiet? no!: it makes 
more sense to *ALWAYS* be sending numbers, so no one knows when 
you are really sending keys. so then, you need to be able to send 
*RANDOM* numbers when you are not sending keys. hopefully, the keys 
look random, and your ``fake'' random numbers look random too! 

trouble: a numbers station near bauta, cuba, around 2007, transmitted 
         some messages with no 9s. innocuous? boring? no! this was a BIG 
         hint that the was filler, and someone messed up! anyway, the 
         fbi or nsa found a similar (possibly the same?) LACK of randomness 
         coming from a cuban numbers station, and then used this information 
         to unleash the expertise of nsa and their subcontractors and catch  
         10 russian spies living under false canadian and us identities. 
         two of these were andrey bezrukov and elena vavilova (pretending to 
         be donald heathfield and tracey lee ann foley), arrested in boston 
         on june 27, 2010. 
