before i tell story #2, i would like to brag/inform a bit: 
 the study of how to simulate randomness, i.e., the study of pseudorandomness, 
 is one of the *DEEPEST* parts of theoretical computer science and mathematics. 
 pseudorandomness has been with us for millenia (gambling?), and since around 
 the 1980s, research in the area has exploded and continues to grow. 

also, an historical note: in 1987, congress debated whether NIST (the 
 national institute of standards and technology, renamed from the national 
 bureau of standards) or nsa would provide security guidance, including 
 cryptographic standards for non-national security agencies. the administration 
 and nsa wanted nsa in charge, but... industry and the public objected, and 
 won... kind of: the computer security act (jan 8, 1988: see also the 
 federal info secu manag act of 2002!) made nist responsible, 
 but *nist was to consult w nsa on the development of cryptographic standards*. 
 from the congressional commmittee report, it is clear congress intended nist 
 be in charge. however, in the ``consultation'' between the agencies, nsa 
 exercised control. e.g., in the 1990s, nist adopted a digital signature 
 standard that was nsa's choice, not industry's. several years later, 
 nist approvl of the clipper keye escrow standard (later dumped) contributed 
 to delay in developing and deploying strong crypto for the private sector. 
 there was a shift in the late 1990s, with the search for a replacement for 
 DES: AES (a belgian-designed standard) came out of an open international 
 competition. however, nsa was playing games behind the scenes: e.g., 
 in dec 2013, reuters reported that nsa paid rsa $10M (when?!) to adopt 
 dual ec-drbg for its default 
 random bit generator in its BSAFE cryptographic toolkit. (nist recommended 
 dropping dual ec-drbg around sep 2013.) however, even after  
 microsoft researchers shumow and ferguson raised objections in 2007, nsa 
 did not deprecate dual ec-drbg!  (see 2014 blog post of sue landau!: 
   https://www.lawfareblog.com/nsas-subversion-nists-algorithm ) 
 
